The canonicalisation in this example is a copy
----------------------------------------------
The two hashes of a message have exactly one right answer, so this example does
not reimplement the way they are computed: the files under
`zeknop_example/canonicalization/` are a copy of the ZEKNOP reference
implementation of the canonical content specification, taken from one fixed
revision of it and carrying this licence header. Apart from that header, the
normalisation of the line endings, and the substitutions recorded with the copy —
each of them inside a comment or a docstring, neither of which the program
branches on; the proof compares the two syntax trees with the docstrings set
aside, so a changed docstring changes `__doc__` and nothing the program computes
— nothing in those files was changed.

The copied files:

    zeknop_example/canonicalization/canonical.py
    zeknop_example/canonicalization/free_form.py
    zeknop_example/canonicalization/mime_walk.py
    zeknop_example/canonicalization/single_byte.py
    zeknop_example/canonicalization/multi_byte.py
    zeknop_example/canonicalization/sig_alg.py
    zeknop_example/canonicalization/message_fields.py

Six of those seven are whole files of the reference implementation.
`message_fields.py` is not: it is five functions of one file of it — the
canonical `From`, the canonical recipients and the text of a Shape A message,
with the two helpers those three use — gathered into a module of their own, and
it is those five functions that were compared, one by one.

`single_byte.py` and `multi_byte.py` are generated from the index data of the
WHATWG Encoding Standard (https://encoding.spec.whatwg.org/), and `free_form.py`
from Unicode character data; each of those files names its own sources in its
header.

Where a comment in the copied files says something was measured — a time, a
memory peak, how two readers disagreed — the measurement is the publisher's,
made while the reference implementation was written, and its evidence is not
part of this package. Those numbers say why a line of code has the shape it has;
they are not a claim about how fast this example is. Where a comment says a
property is held by a test or a gate, that test is the publisher's too.

That the copy answers what the reference implementation answers is not a promise
made here: the `conformance` command runs it over every vector the preview
publishes, and `../../vectors/CONFORMANCE.md` says what a matching report means and
what it does not.

Everything else in this directory is written for this example.
